    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d72">

                <pb facs="0205" n="193" sameAs="frus:frus1946v09/pg193"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">Marshall Mission Files, Lot 54–D270</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">General Chou En-lai
                        to General Marshall</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 103.1–4</seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Chungking</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1946-01-23">23 January 1946</date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">My Dear General Marshall</hi>: With reference to the
                    recommendation of the conference of three on 14 January that the Military
                    Sub-Committee be convened immediately to develop a plan for the reorganization
                    of the Armies of China at the earliest possible date, I have the honor to inform
                    you that Chairman Mao Tze-tung fully <pb facs="0206" n="194" xml:id="pg194"/>
                    approves this recommendation and has made due arrangement to facilitate the
                    proceeding of the Sub-Committee. He further advises me to intimidate [<hi
                        rendition="#italic">intimate</hi>] to you that he entirely subscribes to the
                    Generalissimo’s suggestion<anchor n="64" xml:id="d72-f1"/> that you should be
                    invited to be the advisor of this Sub-Committee.</p>
                <p>Faithfully yours,</p>
                <closer>[Signature in Chinese]<lb/><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Chou
                            En-lai</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d72-f1" n="64" xml:id="d72-f1-content">
                <p>See Generalissimo Chiang’s memorandum to General Marshall, January 16, <ref
                        target="#pg191">p. 191</ref>.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
